  "knowledgePattern": "Use symmetry and split a modulus before integrating",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find where the modulus changes",
      "body": "<p>Solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. These are the places where you may need a different formula for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The sign inside the modulus decides whether to reverse it.",
      "pitfall": "Do not integrate through a sign change using one formula.",
      "pause": "Mark the signs of 1−x² between the integration limits.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l21",
          "reminder": "Split an integral wherever the absolute-value formula changes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Calculate one half first",
      "body": "<p>Check that replacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> unchanged. Then work from 0 to 2.</p>",
      "recap": "Equal left and right halves let you double one half.",
      "pitfall": "On each piece, expand the correct formula before integrating.",
      "pause": "Integrate the pieces from 0 to 2 and use symmetry for the whole interval.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "Use the power rule, then upper-limit value minus lower-limit value."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The integrand is even, so the integral is twice its value from 0 to 2. The modulus changes formula at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on that half-interval.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>, with integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, with integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Doubling their sum gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so D. The integrand is nonnegative throughout; a negative total would signal a sign mistake.</p>"
}
